00:08:26.441 --> 00:08:31.580
So in addition to the real-time
work that I showed you,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:37.180
this part of the talk zeros in on
our social media analytics work.

00:08:37.181 --> 00:08:41.620
So this is something I do with
my young colleague current Sika.

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:45.070
This is like of those four groups,

00:08:45.071 --> 00:08:46.435
this is yet another group,

00:08:46.436 --> 00:08:49.010
basically just the two of us.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:53.290
So are interested in

00:08:53.291 --> 00:08:55.840
social media analytics from
the government side comes

00:08:55.841 --> 00:08:58.150
from detecting people
who are trying to

00:08:58.151 --> 00:09:01.060
recruit for extremist causes,

00:09:01.061 --> 00:09:03.580
and so we look for extremist content,

00:09:03.581 --> 00:09:05.500
extremist behavior and so forth.

00:09:05.501 --> 00:09:07.765
That's why the government
is interested,

00:09:07.766 --> 00:09:10.480
and the key is that
these platforms have

00:09:10.481 --> 00:09:12.910
two things that make
them really stand out.

00:09:12.911 --> 00:09:14.885
One is the sheer volume,

00:09:14.886 --> 00:09:22.875
and the other is the complete
lack of constraints,

00:09:22.876 --> 00:09:25.620
meaning that people can put
whatever content they like.

00:09:25.621 --> 00:09:28.210
People will even may
put a day's worth of

00:09:28.211 --> 00:09:32.170
Fitbit data on social media,
anything goes right.

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:35.065
So it's truly
multi-modal, truly vast,

00:09:35.066 --> 00:09:37.840
and so what it does
is that it stretches

00:09:37.841 --> 00:09:40.960
the boundaries of whatever
standard problems you want to solve.

00:09:40.961 --> 00:09:42.760
You're going to solve a much

00:09:42.761 --> 00:09:46.120
more magnified version
of that problem.

00:09:46.121 --> 00:09:49.960
So that is the way of

00:09:49.961 --> 00:09:52.570
motivation and platforms that

00:09:52.571 --> 00:09:55.495
were looked at include
Twitter, Instagram.

00:09:55.496 --> 00:09:57.670
They are put Facebook
over there but no,

00:09:57.671 --> 00:10:00.115
we don't have access to Facebook but

00:10:00.116 --> 00:10:04.255
surprisingly YouTube
functions as a social medium.

00:10:04.256 --> 00:10:06.190
The next time you go to YouTube,

00:10:06.191 --> 00:10:09.295
just keep this observation in mind.

00:10:09.296 --> 00:10:11.440
Now the problem that
we were interested in

00:10:11.441 --> 00:10:13.570
was modeling content reaction.

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:15.250
So given a piece of content,

00:10:15.251 --> 00:10:17.540
who would be interested in it,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.860
and given a group of users,

00:10:20.861 --> 00:10:23.275
what kind of content would
they be interested in?

00:10:23.276 --> 00:10:25.615
We were interested in
this problem because,

00:10:25.616 --> 00:10:27.670
if you are trying to
track extremist activity,

00:10:27.671 --> 00:10:29.440
when you see extremist content,

00:10:29.441 --> 00:10:31.240
you should know which
group is going to be

00:10:31.241 --> 00:10:33.745
triggered by this kind
of content right.

00:10:33.746 --> 00:10:37.270
So that now obviously on the
commercial side it has an application

00:10:37.271 --> 00:10:42.295
to advertising and so
forth, but for now.

00:10:42.296 --> 00:10:46.090
So more on the technical side,

00:10:46.091 --> 00:10:49.134
then what we are
interested in is taking

00:10:49.135 --> 00:10:53.410
the modalities of text and images,

00:10:53.411 --> 00:10:55.270
and then projecting them into

00:10:55.271 --> 00:10:58.255
a common multimodal
semantic embedding space.

00:10:58.256 --> 00:10:59.860
So basically you can say this is

00:10:59.861 --> 00:11:03.160
a multimodal generalization of
embedding such as word2vec.

00:11:03.161 --> 00:11:04.720
You can just see that
that's pretty much

00:11:04.721 --> 00:11:06.805
almost exactly what's
happening there.

00:11:06.806 --> 00:11:09.910
That you have paired data,

00:11:09.911 --> 00:11:12.085
and the pairing is what is important,

00:11:12.086 --> 00:11:15.160
so now why we are interested in
applying it to social media,

00:11:15.161 --> 00:11:18.190
is that this is a highly
unsupervised kind of technique.

00:11:18.191 --> 00:11:20.290
So in social media that is good,

00:11:20.291 --> 00:11:22.420
because getting labeled
social media data

00:11:22.421 --> 00:11:25.825
is basically not possible,

00:11:25.826 --> 00:11:29.500
and social media has
inherent pairing.

00:11:29.501 --> 00:11:34.325
So those are two things
that encourage us to.

00:11:34.326 --> 00:11:36.780
So now there are
some research questions

00:11:36.781 --> 00:11:41.610
associated with
multi-modal embedding.

00:11:41.611 --> 00:11:46.840
So you have seen work I'm
sure on zeros short learning,

00:11:46.841 --> 00:11:49.060
on captioning, visual
question answering,

00:11:49.061 --> 00:11:52.480
learning better, Word
embeddings and so forth.

00:11:52.481 --> 00:11:58.180
So now, our interest in this came
from the social media analytics.

00:11:58.181 --> 00:12:00.970
So we were interested in first of all

00:12:00.971 --> 00:12:04.315
seeing if we could push Zero short
learning in a new direction,

00:12:04.316 --> 00:12:07.090
and we are also interested

00:12:07.091 --> 00:12:09.340
in finding out if you could

00:12:09.341 --> 00:12:12.010
embed the users and
the content in the same space,

00:12:12.011 --> 00:12:14.785
to treat users as
an additional modality,

00:12:14.786 --> 00:12:17.110
and then have a three-way embedding,

00:12:17.111 --> 00:12:18.430
so that I could have

00:12:18.431 --> 00:12:21.805
seamless retrieval between
all of those modalities.

00:12:21.806 --> 00:12:24.010
So that led us to

00:12:24.011 --> 00:12:27.505
first this problem called
Zero short object detection.

00:12:27.506 --> 00:12:33.145
So this is something we
published it easy CV 2018.

00:12:33.146 --> 00:12:37.150
This came out of a project that we

00:12:37.151 --> 00:12:40.855
started as a summer intern under
the darpa metaphor project,

00:12:40.856 --> 00:12:44.440
and what we're interested in here is,

00:12:44.441 --> 00:12:47.530
the not merely object classification.

00:12:47.531 --> 00:12:50.440
We want to also localize the object,

00:12:50.441 --> 00:12:53.170
and it should be previously unseen.

00:12:53.171 --> 00:12:58.510
So I'll just flip through
these in the interest of time.

00:12:58.511 --> 00:13:02.980
This is just some
background material right,

00:13:02.981 --> 00:13:06.730
that when you want to.

00:13:06.731 --> 00:13:08.680
This is basically saying that,

00:13:08.681 --> 00:13:11.110
there are these existing
techniques like YOLO,

00:13:11.111 --> 00:13:14.095
Faster, R-CNN, SSD etc.

00:13:14.096 --> 00:13:17.620
But scaling beyond
a few added objects and

00:13:17.621 --> 00:13:19.630
detecting completely new objects

00:13:19.631 --> 00:13:22.150
is still beyond the reach of
these kinds of techniques.

00:13:22.151 --> 00:13:27.860
So now, what is Zero short learning?

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:30.880
I'm going to state the obvious just

00:13:30.881 --> 00:13:32.950
so at least we have
a common terminology.

00:13:32.951 --> 00:13:35.410
So, the idea is that,

00:13:35.411 --> 00:13:37.780
you learn from seen classes but

00:13:37.781 --> 00:13:40.360
then you predict on unseen classes.

00:13:40.361 --> 00:13:42.205
So that is the you can say

00:13:42.206 --> 00:13:46.900
a good definition of
what Zero short classification.

00:13:46.901 --> 00:13:49.810
So then the question is,
what is Zero shot detection?

00:13:49.811 --> 00:13:53.005
So this illustration is
telling you what that is.

00:13:53.006 --> 00:13:55.600
Detection is that in
addition to saying

00:13:55.601 --> 00:13:58.090
that there is a cat in the picture,

00:13:58.091 --> 00:14:00.070
you're also saying
exactly where the cat

00:14:00.071 --> 00:14:02.185
is or exactly where the dog is right,

00:14:02.186 --> 00:14:07.640
and now you're going to do
this in a Zero shot fashion.

00:14:07.641 --> 00:14:11.270
Now all the other problems
such as the occlusion,

00:14:11.271 --> 00:14:15.300
viewpoint, lateral etc,
still come in play.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.550
There are some certainly
obvious applications

00:14:19.551 --> 00:14:23.190
like robotics and surveillance.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:26.900
The question is, do we
really need thousands or

00:14:26.901 --> 00:14:29.555
training examples to deal
with a new category?

00:14:29.556 --> 00:14:33.155
Our answer based on what
we've looked at, is no.

00:14:33.156 --> 00:14:37.560
So this is just a sketch of
our approach to this problem.

00:14:37.561 --> 00:14:41.500
So first of all

00:14:41.501 --> 00:14:45.395
we want to take previous work
in zeros short learning.

00:14:45.396 --> 00:14:48.035
You don't want to create
something out of whole cloth.

00:14:48.036 --> 00:14:49.970
But then the task is,

00:14:49.971 --> 00:14:52.590
how do we model the background.

00:14:52.591 --> 00:14:54.160
So what happens is that,

00:14:54.161 --> 00:14:57.045
when you have all only
previously seen objects,

00:14:57.046 --> 00:14:59.705
then the definition of
background become very clear.

00:14:59.706 --> 00:15:02.450
Because it is whatever is

00:15:02.451 --> 00:15:06.245
not in the class of seen
objects is the background.

00:15:06.246 --> 00:15:09.605
The moment you have objects
that you've never seen before,

00:15:09.606 --> 00:15:11.300
now there's a new class.

00:15:11.301 --> 00:15:13.010
So there is the typical background,

00:15:13.011 --> 00:15:16.340
which you could call a
stuff class like sky or

00:15:16.341 --> 00:15:20.790
a wall or water etc.

00:15:20.791 --> 00:15:23.780
But now you have a class
of objects that you have

00:15:23.781 --> 00:15:26.900
not seen before and that may
also merge into the background.

00:15:26.901 --> 00:15:29.165
So that presents
a bit of a challenge,

00:15:29.166 --> 00:15:32.315
and you will now see how we

00:15:32.316 --> 00:15:36.275
have dealt with this kind of
problem as we go forward,

00:15:36.276 --> 00:15:39.525
and then what we've
also done is that,

00:15:39.526 --> 00:15:42.295
you know you'll never have
enough training data,

00:15:42.296 --> 00:15:45.399
that is for sure, for
any problem of this nature,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:49.605
just because it is so big in scale.

00:15:49.606 --> 00:15:52.665
So what we have done is

00:15:52.666 --> 00:15:56.210
explored this business of dense
sampling of the semantic space.

00:15:56.211 --> 00:16:00.020
So wherever you are certain,

00:16:00.021 --> 00:16:04.895
what you do is that you try to
sample close to that and make.

00:16:04.896 --> 00:16:07.430
So even though you don't
have labeled data,

00:16:07.431 --> 00:16:08.750
but you try to sample over

00:16:08.751 --> 00:16:10.580
there and try to make
the embedding very

00:16:10.581 --> 00:16:16.780
dense around that
particular location.

00:16:16.781 --> 00:16:21.030
So now, what is our baseline method?

00:16:21.031 --> 00:16:24.830
We use is R-CNN architecture but
that's really not the point.

00:16:24.831 --> 00:16:26.570
I mean, you could use

00:16:26.571 --> 00:16:30.795
any other architecture also
for this kind of method,

00:16:30.796 --> 00:16:33.290
and we are just showing you some sort

00:16:33.291 --> 00:16:36.290
of you can say, already some result.

00:16:36.291 --> 00:16:37.730
Well, what we've done
is we've carried

00:16:37.731 --> 00:16:40.640
out training on the scene classes,

00:16:40.641 --> 00:16:44.190
and then when we test
on the unseen classes.

00:16:44.191 --> 00:16:48.585
So shoulder and skirt were part
of the set of unseen classes,

00:16:48.586 --> 00:16:50.840
and you see that this
actually does in fact

00:16:50.841 --> 00:16:54.655
detect the skirt and the shoulder,

00:16:54.656 --> 00:16:58.885
presumably because in
the world embeddings,

00:16:58.886 --> 00:17:03.125
there was enough similarity
scene between say skirt and

00:17:03.126 --> 00:17:07.100
shirt or proximity between short
and shoulder and so forth,

00:17:07.101 --> 00:17:09.080
and then there are
some joint learning

00:17:09.081 --> 00:17:10.730
of the visual attributes as well,

00:17:10.731 --> 00:17:13.500
which managed to give
you this kind of result.

00:17:13.501 --> 00:17:15.520
&gt;&gt; So I guess the idea is that,

00:17:15.521 --> 00:17:17.454
in this embedding space,

00:17:17.455 --> 00:17:19.190
all these concepts will be

00:17:19.191 --> 00:17:21.290
nearest neighbors
versus other concepts.

00:17:21.291 --> 00:17:23.330
&gt;&gt; That's right. So
the idea is that when

00:17:23.331 --> 00:17:25.370
you're train this embeddings,
what you are trying to do is,

00:17:25.371 --> 00:17:26.870
you're trying to pull similar things

00:17:26.871 --> 00:17:30.290
together and you're trying to
pull dissimilar things apart.

00:17:30.291 --> 00:17:32.855
So you are depending very
heavily on the pairing.

00:17:32.856 --> 00:17:36.330
If it did not have paired data
and embedding would not work.

00:17:36.331 --> 00:17:40.915
&gt;&gt; What is the testing scenario
like in object detection?

00:17:40.916 --> 00:17:45.650
It's basically forming
boxes being embedded.

00:17:45.651 --> 00:17:49.140
In this case will you say
testing on unseen classes?

00:17:49.141 --> 00:17:51.460
Is it exactly the same?

00:17:51.461 --> 00:17:52.555
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:52.556 --> 00:17:54.970
&gt;&gt; They're bonding boxes
and they're being labeled.

00:17:54.971 --> 00:17:56.080
&gt;&gt; That's right. There are

00:17:56.081 --> 00:17:57.460
bonding boxes and they
are being labeled,

00:17:57.461 --> 00:17:58.790
and then you say that okay,

00:17:58.791 --> 00:18:00.680
I was able to see that
there is this skirt there,

00:18:00.681 --> 00:18:02.270
even though it was not part of.

00:18:02.271 --> 00:18:04.190
I didn't train on skirts.

00:18:04.191 --> 00:18:06.840
Now on the lighter side,

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:11.750
at another place where
I gave this talk,

00:18:11.751 --> 00:18:13.730
a young lady in the audience
pointed out that

00:18:13.731 --> 00:18:18.460
this not a skirt that's a dress.

00:18:18.461 --> 00:18:19.975
So what I always say is,

00:18:19.976 --> 00:18:21.080
that's a reflection on

00:18:21.081 --> 00:18:24.620
the gender composition of
the people who made this algorithm.

00:18:24.621 --> 00:18:27.110
&gt;&gt; The sizes of the bounding box are

00:18:27.111 --> 00:18:30.200
basically whatever
these networks generate.

00:18:30.201 --> 00:18:32.490
&gt;&gt; Yes. That's right.

00:18:32.491 --> 00:18:35.750
&gt;&gt; So they are both backup words
and back up boxes right,

00:18:35.751 --> 00:18:37.490
so there is no structural information

00:18:37.491 --> 00:18:39.750
embedded into the system right now.

00:18:39.751 --> 00:18:42.010
So what would prevent, let's say,

00:18:42.011 --> 00:18:44.290
shoulder getting
detected as skirt and

00:18:44.291 --> 00:18:47.065
vice versa because there
is a lot of similarity?

00:18:47.066 --> 00:18:50.080
It's [inaudible].

00:18:50.081 --> 00:18:52.700
&gt;&gt; No, that is very true.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:19:02.545
So with these deep learning systems,

00:19:02.546 --> 00:19:06.085
you can come only so close
to giving some kind of

00:19:06.086 --> 00:19:09.190
an intuitive answer after
the fact I could try

00:19:09.191 --> 00:19:12.115
to tell you that somehow,

00:19:12.116 --> 00:19:15.175
this fabric need to be a bit floppy.

00:19:15.176 --> 00:19:18.790
Overall, the nature of
the relationship between fabric and

00:19:18.791 --> 00:19:23.185
skin with lower body attire is
different from upper body attire.

00:19:23.186 --> 00:19:25.780
So if you have enough
of training data,

00:19:25.781 --> 00:19:27.280
that kind of thing will be learned.

00:19:27.281 --> 00:19:28.270
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:19:28.271 --> 00:19:29.680
&gt;&gt; But having said that,

00:19:29.681 --> 00:19:32.020
I'm not telling you that that
is the answer to the question.

00:19:32.021 --> 00:19:35.740
It's an educated guess,

00:19:35.741 --> 00:19:37.090
I would say, frankly.

00:19:37.091 --> 00:19:37.630
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:19:37.631 --> 00:19:41.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So that's the general idea.

00:19:41.441 --> 00:19:45.970
Now, here are
some mathematical details

00:19:45.971 --> 00:19:47.290
here that what you're doing is,

00:19:47.291 --> 00:19:53.930
you first trained

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.610
the embedding in advance,

00:19:57.611 --> 00:20:01.540
and now, you are projecting it
using the linear projection.

00:20:01.541 --> 00:20:03.355
Then, what you are asking me,

00:20:03.356 --> 00:20:06.610
you are taking the box and then
comparing it to the label.

00:20:06.611 --> 00:20:12.805
So basically, it's a box
by box result, as it were.

00:20:12.806 --> 00:20:14.620
Then, what you are doing is,

00:20:14.621 --> 00:20:18.640
you are pushing embedding for
similar boxes and class labels,

00:20:18.641 --> 00:20:21.985
you're trying to ram them
together, and so forth.

00:20:21.986 --> 00:20:26.260
So now, the interesting
part comes in,

00:20:26.261 --> 00:20:28.660
which is one of the innovations
in this approach,

00:20:28.661 --> 00:20:31.585
which is what allowed
the solution to the problem.

00:20:31.586 --> 00:20:37.810
So what detection models

00:20:37.811 --> 00:20:39.670
with a fixed number of
classes do is that they will

00:20:39.671 --> 00:20:42.010
add an additional background class.

00:20:42.011 --> 00:20:44.650
That's like some kind of
container class that just

00:20:44.651 --> 00:20:47.230
takes care of whatever is
not in the same classes.

00:20:47.231 --> 00:20:48.655
This is not like that.

00:20:48.656 --> 00:20:56.875
So when we try to just use
a single background class,

00:20:56.876 --> 00:21:00.610
we end up not getting very good
results in certain cases.

00:21:00.611 --> 00:21:03.130
The reason for that is that

00:21:03.131 --> 00:21:08.065
these previously unseen objects
merge into the background.

00:21:08.066 --> 00:21:10.300
So they get classified as background,

00:21:10.301 --> 00:21:12.130
and they are not seen at all.

00:21:12.131 --> 00:21:15.730
So what we have done
basically is come up with

00:21:15.731 --> 00:21:19.540
a way that assigns latent classes.

00:21:19.541 --> 00:21:21.790
So it allows latent
classes to emerge,

00:21:21.791 --> 00:21:23.770
these are not labeled.

00:21:23.771 --> 00:21:26.830
But what you do is, you
make a distinction between

00:21:26.831 --> 00:21:31.780
background areas that are
just stuff and background areas

00:21:31.781 --> 00:21:35.710
that actually correspond
to some latent classes

00:21:35.711 --> 00:21:40.390
that may or may not correspond
to previously unseen objects.

00:21:40.391 --> 00:21:44.440
So once you do this kind of a thing,

00:21:44.441 --> 00:21:47.470
we find that we are, in fact,

00:21:47.471 --> 00:21:51.880
able to get better results.

00:21:51.881 --> 00:21:54.160
Now, in the densely embedded space,

00:21:54.161 --> 00:21:56.185
what we're doing is,

00:21:56.186 --> 00:22:02.110
the issue is that this embedding
space can be very sparse,

00:22:02.111 --> 00:22:04.885
you have no control over the density.

00:22:04.886 --> 00:22:07.600
We all know that any kind of
interpolation method will

00:22:07.601 --> 00:22:10.030
work well if it is being
done in a dense region,

00:22:10.031 --> 00:22:12.010
you want the points to be dense.

00:22:12.011 --> 00:22:14.170
So what we have done
over here is basically,

00:22:14.171 --> 00:22:19.165
we augmented the training dataset
with just additional classes.

00:22:19.166 --> 00:22:22.840
There is no overlap with
the unseen classes,

00:22:22.841 --> 00:22:25.735
we do our best to not cheat.

00:22:25.736 --> 00:22:28.630
But then, what you are
trying to do basically is,

00:22:28.631 --> 00:22:29.695
you are trying to take

00:22:29.696 --> 00:22:32.530
whatever areas you know
and you want to pack

00:22:32.531 --> 00:22:37.240
them with more data so that
those areas become denser.

00:22:37.241 --> 00:22:40.720
So now, what we have done is that we

00:22:40.721 --> 00:22:44.875
have worked with the MSCOCO
and Visual Genome dataset.

00:22:44.876 --> 00:22:48.610
Now, we did this split,
where, with MSCOCO,

00:22:48.611 --> 00:22:51.385
we had total of 65,

00:22:51.386 --> 00:22:54.280
so 48 seen and 17 unseen.

00:22:54.281 --> 00:22:57.505
Visual Genome has more classes in it,

00:22:57.506 --> 00:23:01.435
and we see an immediate
consequence of this.

00:23:01.436 --> 00:23:04.590
Now, these are some more details,

00:23:04.591 --> 00:23:05.820
but I think in the interest of time,

00:23:05.821 --> 00:23:07.440
I can skip some of these details.

00:23:07.441 --> 00:23:14.100
But the idea basically is
that this is, you can say,

00:23:14.101 --> 00:23:15.840
our experimental framework, this is

00:23:15.841 --> 00:23:22.630
the framework that we're
using to run these tests.

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:27.285
So what happens is that
for the Visual Genome,

00:23:27.286 --> 00:23:30.240
having this kind of procedure
where you are letting

00:23:30.241 --> 00:23:33.790
these latent classes emerge in
the background actually helps,

00:23:33.791 --> 00:23:35.725
and you get the best results.

00:23:35.726 --> 00:23:40.030
That is not quite evident
in the MSCOCO case,

00:23:40.031 --> 00:23:44.950
and that is our theory is that
that is because in Visual Genome,

00:23:44.951 --> 00:23:46.960
you have a larger number of objects.

00:23:46.961 --> 00:23:48.970
Presumably, when you
have a larger number

00:23:48.971 --> 00:23:50.830
of objects to present
a greater challenge,

00:23:50.831 --> 00:23:55.420
and that is when some benefit
is seen for this kind of

00:23:55.421 --> 00:24:02.420
method of having a latent
background classes, and so forth.

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:06.685
So here is some more now.

00:24:06.686 --> 00:24:08.530
There are some good classes,

00:24:08.531 --> 00:24:10.150
and there are some bad classes.

00:24:10.151 --> 00:24:12.320
These are, obviously,

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:15.760
you-all agree with
me that mistakes are

00:24:15.761 --> 00:24:18.250
more interesting than
what you get right.

00:24:18.251 --> 00:24:20.530
So when you look at this,

00:24:20.531 --> 00:24:22.120
so you see, for example,

00:24:22.121 --> 00:24:24.055
over here that it gets zebra wrong.

00:24:24.056 --> 00:24:28.750
Later on, you will see it
persistently called zebra some.

00:24:28.751 --> 00:24:32.665
It just somehow does
not recognize zebras,

00:24:32.666 --> 00:24:37.060
whereas you saw that it's able
to detect things like skirts,

00:24:37.061 --> 00:24:40.360
and buildings, and
cakes, chairs, so forth.

00:24:40.361 --> 00:24:48.160
So, yeah, this is little more detail,

00:24:48.161 --> 00:24:51.535
but I'll skip ahead to
some visual results.

00:24:51.536 --> 00:24:55.945
So most of these results
are as expected.

00:24:55.946 --> 00:24:59.065
For example, this result
is fairly obvious,

00:24:59.066 --> 00:25:01.150
you know it is picking up a bus.

00:25:01.151 --> 00:25:03.850
My favorite result is this one,

00:25:03.851 --> 00:25:07.870
I made a double-take with this one.

00:25:07.871 --> 00:25:11.365
I first thought it was a dog myself,

00:25:11.366 --> 00:25:13.240
but it's actually a cat,

00:25:13.241 --> 00:25:16.280
but this system thinks it's a dog.

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:23.830
Hongjiang Zhang used
to be with Microsoft,

00:25:23.831 --> 00:25:27.550
and he once told me that when
your system makes a mistake,

00:25:27.551 --> 00:25:30.220
you feel that, okay,
it's real, it's not.

00:25:30.221 --> 00:25:32.440
So this is a fascinating mistake,

00:25:32.441 --> 00:25:34.570
in the sense that I think that it's

00:25:34.571 --> 00:25:38.290
the pointy ears that somehow
this thing is learning,

00:25:38.291 --> 00:25:40.855
and it thinks that it's a dog.

00:25:40.856 --> 00:25:43.660
But for the most part,

00:25:43.661 --> 00:25:44.830
it's not bad at all,

00:25:44.831 --> 00:25:48.235
it's actually getting
a lot of these things.

00:25:48.236 --> 00:25:52.390
Now, here, you see [inaudible].

00:25:52.391 --> 00:25:53.605
You had this question, right?

00:25:53.606 --> 00:25:55.420
Look, it made exactly that mistake.

00:25:55.421 --> 00:25:57.460
See? Do you think that it's a skirt?

00:25:57.461 --> 00:26:00.040
It saw the fabric and skin boundary,

00:26:00.041 --> 00:26:02.770
and it thinks it's
a skirt, but it's not.

00:26:02.771 --> 00:26:04.510
So it does get those things

00:26:04.511 --> 00:26:08.260
wrong probably because
the thing that you pointed out,

00:26:08.261 --> 00:26:13.075
there is no structural
information captured.

00:26:13.076 --> 00:26:19.075
Now, it persistently thinks that
it has found cattle over here.

00:26:19.076 --> 00:26:22.705
At some level, this is reassuring.

00:26:22.706 --> 00:26:24.295
In fact, later on,

00:26:24.296 --> 00:26:26.770
you will see we have tried
some metrics where what

00:26:26.771 --> 00:26:29.440
I encourage my folks to think

00:26:29.441 --> 00:26:31.660
about was that maybe you think of

00:26:31.661 --> 00:26:34.990
an ontology and try to look for
similarity one level above.

00:26:34.991 --> 00:26:38.080
So probably mistaking
zebra for cattle is not as

00:26:38.081 --> 00:26:41.515
bad as mistaking zebra
for a door knob.

00:26:41.516 --> 00:26:44.410
That's a much more serious mistake.

00:26:44.411 --> 00:26:47.020
So there are levels at
which you can test how

00:26:47.021 --> 00:26:49.900
well this kind of
technique is working.

00:26:49.901 --> 00:26:52.975
&gt;&gt; All those categories were
not there in the training data.

00:26:52.976 --> 00:26:53.650
&gt;&gt; No.

00:26:53.651 --> 00:26:56.380
&gt;&gt; None of them were. Were
there any cases where they were

00:26:56.381 --> 00:26:58.885
overlapping in the two datasets,

00:26:58.886 --> 00:27:02.860
but somehow, the domain would
be very different, right?

00:27:02.861 --> 00:27:04.570
Let's say, the cat's.

00:27:04.571 --> 00:27:05.785
&gt;&gt; To my knowledge, no.

00:27:05.786 --> 00:27:06.360
&gt;&gt; No?

00:27:06.361 --> 00:27:07.465
&gt;&gt; To my knowledge, not,

00:27:07.466 --> 00:27:09.925
but I'd have to go
back and check that.

00:27:09.926 --> 00:27:12.580
&gt;&gt; If you would allow a few labels,

00:27:12.581 --> 00:27:15.265
will the [inaudible] as a second one?

00:27:15.266 --> 00:27:18.370
&gt;&gt; If I allow a few,

00:27:18.371 --> 00:27:19.075
&gt;&gt; Few labels.

00:27:19.076 --> 00:27:22.345
&gt;&gt; Right. So that's
a very good question.

00:27:22.346 --> 00:27:24.040
I have wrestled with
that all the time

00:27:24.041 --> 00:27:25.690
I worked with machine learning.

00:27:25.691 --> 00:27:30.220
What I've found is that
it's hard to say I mean,

00:27:30.221 --> 00:27:34.375
he did try, current tried
some talk and type experiments.

00:27:34.376 --> 00:27:40.840
So we did get some results
like that back there.

00:27:40.841 --> 00:27:46.960
&gt;&gt; But it's also possible that
what your calling unseen concepts,

00:27:46.961 --> 00:27:47.350
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:27:47.351 --> 00:27:49.510
&gt;&gt; What you're saying unseen
is in the visual domain,

00:27:49.511 --> 00:27:51.700
but they're there in
text domain, right?

00:27:51.701 --> 00:27:52.540
&gt;&gt; No, absolutely.

00:27:52.541 --> 00:27:52.735
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:27:52.736 --> 00:27:54.040
&gt;&gt; So they are not unread.

00:27:54.041 --> 00:27:56.080
They are unseen, but
they're not unread.

00:27:56.081 --> 00:27:59.320
&gt;&gt; So zebra might not have
been there much at all,

00:27:59.321 --> 00:28:01.360
in either one of them in which case.

00:28:01.361 --> 00:28:03.385
&gt;&gt; In which case, it goes
of course like this.

00:28:03.386 --> 00:28:07.045
It's still fascinating that it's
somehow thought it was cattle.

00:28:07.046 --> 00:28:09.520
But again, I won't read
too much into that.

00:28:09.521 --> 00:28:13.180
Who knows what it latched on to,

00:28:13.181 --> 00:28:15.445
we don't necessarily know.

00:28:15.446 --> 00:28:18.850
This actually gives us the cue
to ask another question,

00:28:18.851 --> 00:28:21.130
so how do we judge
the success of an embedding?

00:28:21.131 --> 00:28:23.260
Because that's our next problem here.

00:28:23.261 --> 00:28:24.970
Because we start off with

00:28:24.971 --> 00:28:28.300
this multi-modal embeddings
for social media now.

00:28:28.301 --> 00:28:31.780
The reason we are interested
in an embedding is that we

00:28:31.781 --> 00:28:35.410
want to solve the problem I
mentioned earlier which is that,

00:28:35.411 --> 00:28:37.060
given a piece of content,

00:28:37.061 --> 00:28:39.100
who would be interested
in this content?

00:28:39.101 --> 00:28:42.580
Given a user, what kind of
content would they be interested?

00:28:42.581 --> 00:28:45.550
We're interested in
solving that problem.

00:28:45.551 --> 00:28:49.030
So yes, we can certainly if
we had a joint embedding,

00:28:49.031 --> 00:28:51.170
that would be very nice.

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:56.125
Now, why are we interested in
this kind of thing specifically?

00:28:56.126 --> 00:28:59.230
There's another reason also
like I was telling you earlier.

00:28:59.231 --> 00:29:02.560
There is absolutely
no constraint in social media.

00:29:02.561 --> 00:29:04.555
Absolutely, anything can pop up.

00:29:04.556 --> 00:29:06.295
So you need to deal with that.

00:29:06.296 --> 00:29:10.480
So it's a much harsher
environment than

00:29:10.481 --> 00:29:18.395
relatively domesticated environments
such as MS COCO or VGG.

00:29:18.396 --> 00:29:21.700
So that's what we are
interested in over here.

00:29:21.701 --> 00:29:30.280
So we come up with this kind

00:29:30.281 --> 00:29:34.150
of embedding technique where
what we're doing is we're

00:29:34.151 --> 00:29:39.910
using images posted by the user
as a stand-in for the user.

00:29:39.911 --> 00:29:42.790
So we really are not, obviously,

00:29:42.791 --> 00:29:44.620
you have to take some user attribute

00:29:44.621 --> 00:29:47.170
and use that as a stand-in
for the embedding.

00:29:47.171 --> 00:29:50.260
You don't literally embed the user.

00:29:50.261 --> 00:29:52.330
So in this case, what
we're doing is we're just

00:29:52.331 --> 00:29:54.985
taking the images that
the user has posted,

00:29:54.986 --> 00:29:58.510
and then embedding those.

00:29:58.511 --> 00:30:01.240
So we're using the same framework
as we did earlier,

00:30:01.241 --> 00:30:05.230
but now we are attaching
this user label with that image so

00:30:05.231 --> 00:30:10.070
that we're maintaining it
as a separate modality.

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:16.600
So now, I'll play you
a video of this demo.

00:30:16.601 --> 00:30:18.310
So now, what this allows you to do

00:30:18.311 --> 00:30:20.380
is carry out a four-way retrieval.

00:30:20.381 --> 00:30:24.010
So we have text, we have users,

00:30:24.011 --> 00:30:26.830
we have images, and
then we have also done

00:30:26.831 --> 00:30:30.460
some unsupervised
clustering of the users.

00:30:30.461 --> 00:30:32.890
So we have user clusters also.

00:30:32.891 --> 00:30:34.990
Again, what we do is that we use

00:30:34.991 --> 00:30:37.915
an image as a stand-in
for the user cluster,

00:30:37.916 --> 00:30:41.950
and we are able to embed user
clusters also in the same framework.

00:30:41.951 --> 00:30:43.930
So what this allows
you to do therefore,

00:30:43.931 --> 00:30:46.270
is that you can
retrieve based on text,

00:30:46.271 --> 00:30:49.675
you can retrieve
the other three modalities.

00:30:49.676 --> 00:30:55.840
So what are the type over there I
can't read, the thing too small.

00:30:55.841 --> 00:30:58.075
Yeah, it says Amazon Rain Forest.

00:30:58.076 --> 00:31:00.730
So you'll get some images
related to that,

00:31:00.731 --> 00:31:02.470
you get the users who are

00:31:02.471 --> 00:31:05.080
interested in that kind
of topic, and so forth.

00:31:05.081 --> 00:31:06.460
Then you can go all over the place.

00:31:06.461 --> 00:31:08.815
I'll play you that video in a moment.

00:31:08.816 --> 00:31:16.330
So there are some details here on
this slide on how the embedding,

00:31:16.331 --> 00:31:19.105
what kind of metrics are
being used, and so forth.

00:31:19.106 --> 00:31:20.710
Now, what we are done here

00:31:20.711 --> 00:31:24.445
is we have taken one month
of Twitter data,

00:31:24.446 --> 00:31:26.860
and we wanted all
this to unsupervised.

00:31:26.861 --> 00:31:30.400
So what we did was, we collected
one month of Twitter data,

00:31:30.401 --> 00:31:36.050
and we took the top few hashtags.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:39.850
So we just chose some
arbitrary numbers if we want,

00:31:39.851 --> 00:31:44.365
because we wanted
some density to that data.

00:31:44.366 --> 00:31:46.450
There's a hashtag with just one entry

00:31:46.451 --> 00:31:48.535
that's going to come
in and just sit there.

00:31:48.536 --> 00:31:50.425
So we got that.

00:31:50.426 --> 00:31:56.010
So we ended up with
about 10-12 million tweets,

00:31:56.011 --> 00:31:59.010
about a million images,
and 40,000 users.

00:31:59.011 --> 00:32:02.350
These are month's worth of data.

00:32:02.750 --> 00:32:07.840
It's surprising what this kind of
thing actually ends up learning.

00:32:07.841 --> 00:32:10.540
Now, this is where we carry

00:32:10.541 --> 00:32:13.210
out the kind of experiments
that you are talking about,

00:32:13.211 --> 00:32:15.085
where what we do is we use ranking.

00:32:15.086 --> 00:32:19.570
We tried to figure out whether
the rank makes sense or not,

00:32:19.571 --> 00:32:21.325
and just a sanity check.

00:32:21.326 --> 00:32:24.610
So we first test only with
text, only with images,

00:32:24.611 --> 00:32:25.690
or only with users,

00:32:25.691 --> 00:32:29.425
and we find where at least
much better than random.

00:32:29.426 --> 00:32:32.920
Then as we start adding modalities,

00:32:32.921 --> 00:32:34.240
as you start pairing them up,

00:32:34.241 --> 00:32:37.855
you see that the rank
starts to improve.

00:32:37.856 --> 00:32:45.320
Then, here are some results
with other variations.

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:50.800
This is in general, it's a bit of

00:32:50.801 --> 00:32:53.200
an interesting problem
that exactly how

00:32:53.201 --> 00:32:56.440
do you evaluate an embedding?

00:32:56.441 --> 00:32:58.420
How do you believe in embedding,

00:32:58.421 --> 00:33:00.310
how do you know what we
will learn, and all that.

00:33:00.311 --> 00:33:02.980
So our operational
definition is that,

00:33:02.981 --> 00:33:06.475
if for our task it gives
results that makes sense,

00:33:06.476 --> 00:33:09.830
then we believe this embedding.

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:17.470
We have a restricted definition
of the efficacy of the- Now,

00:33:17.471 --> 00:33:20.155
you see that we have played around
with different kinds of things.

00:33:20.156 --> 00:33:21.790
You can use it with GloVe,

00:33:21.791 --> 00:33:25.885
which is pre-trained on a very
large corpus or you can limit it.

00:33:25.886 --> 00:33:29.350
Because there are some interesting
aspects on the tech side also.

00:33:29.351 --> 00:33:31.405
The interesting aspects are,

00:33:31.406 --> 00:33:33.730
that the kind of language you see in

00:33:33.731 --> 00:33:36.745
social media is very
different from what you see

00:33:36.746 --> 00:33:39.190
in the formal document
that things like

00:33:39.191 --> 00:33:41.980
GloVe and Word2Vec
have been trained on.

00:33:41.981 --> 00:33:43.885
So lots of informal language,

00:33:43.886 --> 00:33:46.660
lots of these so-called
stopper words.

00:33:46.661 --> 00:33:49.840
So you have to do some cleaning
processing and all that.

00:33:49.841 --> 00:33:54.685
So there is that on
that side of things.

00:33:54.686 --> 00:33:57.400
So here, there are

00:33:57.401 --> 00:34:01.825
some more detailed results
where the joint model,

00:34:01.826 --> 00:34:04.180
are you using a GRU instead of

00:34:04.181 --> 00:34:09.745
a convolutional network,
and so forth.

00:34:09.746 --> 00:34:12.700
The user clusters that
I showed you what we

00:34:12.701 --> 00:34:15.220
do is that, those are unsupervised.

00:34:15.221 --> 00:34:16.720
But we find that interestingly,

00:34:16.721 --> 00:34:18.850
these clusters are
actually seem to make

00:34:18.851 --> 00:34:21.040
some sort of semantic sense.

00:34:21.041 --> 00:34:26.875
For example, this seems to
be around British politics,

00:34:26.876 --> 00:34:29.110
this box over here.

00:34:29.111 --> 00:34:31.630
The other ones we
have put some images,

00:34:31.631 --> 00:34:33.700
so you see soccer images and

00:34:33.701 --> 00:34:39.080
soccer related themes are sort of
falling together, and so forth.

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:46.585
So basically,

00:34:46.586 --> 00:34:52.105
what we are doing right now is we
are trying to write all this up.

00:34:52.106 --> 00:34:55.630
Then, the ECC part of course,

00:34:55.631 --> 00:34:59.065
is done now with this part
with the social media.

00:34:59.066 --> 00:35:03.760
We are waiting for the right
kind of forum to publish this.

00:35:03.761 --> 00:35:05.140
What I'll do is,

00:35:05.141 --> 00:35:08.875
I will quickly play you a video.

00:35:08.876 --> 00:35:13.400
Then, I'll hand over to
you to talk to you about.

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:20.170
So I already told
you about this data.

00:35:20.171 --> 00:35:22.780
This is one month of
Twitter data and so forth.

00:35:22.781 --> 00:35:25.210
Now, see he types in over there,

00:35:25.211 --> 00:35:27.800
he'll type in the box.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:32.830
He types in say, "vegan food".

00:35:32.831 --> 00:35:35.710
So when he types in vegan
food with the embedding,

00:35:35.711 --> 00:35:37.105
he gets these retrieval.

00:35:37.106 --> 00:35:39.340
So it's interesting
that one month of Twitter

00:35:39.341 --> 00:35:41.830
seems to have images
related to vegan food.

00:35:41.831 --> 00:35:44.425
&gt;&gt; So these embeddings
have used labels

00:35:44.426 --> 00:35:47.785
associated with images when
the embeddings are created?

00:35:47.786 --> 00:35:51.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, basically captions.

00:35:51.041 --> 00:35:51.715
&gt;&gt; Captions.

00:35:51.716 --> 00:35:55.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Captions
sometimes, it's loose also,

00:35:55.271 --> 00:35:56.800
if there are no caption but there is

00:35:56.801 --> 00:36:00.310
a some kind of text
associated with that tweet.

00:36:00.311 --> 00:36:03.355
That's the difficulty
with this kind of data.

00:36:03.356 --> 00:36:04.450
But now you see that it is

00:36:04.451 --> 00:36:06.880
doing retrieval from
all over the place.

00:36:06.881 --> 00:36:09.385
Retrieval starting on the image side,

00:36:09.386 --> 00:36:11.860
a retrieval against
starting on the text side,

00:36:11.861 --> 00:36:15.070
but also from the user side
or the user cluster side.

00:36:15.071 --> 00:36:17.980
So we are done this kind of

00:36:17.981 --> 00:36:20.800
work where we are interested
only in rabble-rousing video.

00:36:20.801 --> 00:36:22.360
We've published that at ICMI,

00:36:22.361 --> 00:36:25.120
but this is a generalization
of that work.

00:36:25.121 --> 00:36:28.390
Now you have a general content model

00:36:28.391 --> 00:36:32.170
which allows you to predict
given a piece of content,

00:36:32.171 --> 00:36:34.300
who would be interested in it.

00:36:34.301 --> 00:36:39.460
So what I'll do is that,

00:36:39.461 --> 00:36:42.655
I will pause over here

00:36:42.656 --> 00:36:46.360
and I can certainly take a couple
of questions and let me,

00:36:46.361 --> 00:36:51.895
&gt;&gt; Are they surprising results
in this which you expect?

00:36:51.896 --> 00:36:53.350
They're plausible.

00:36:53.351 --> 00:36:56.650
&gt;&gt; Though surprisingly

00:36:56.651 --> 00:36:58.750
those are kind of topics that popup.

00:36:58.751 --> 00:37:00.835
For example, we typed in fashion

00:37:00.836 --> 00:37:03.850
not quite knowing what to expect
and we're very surprised,

00:37:03.851 --> 00:37:06.565
you type in some designer
or something.

00:37:06.566 --> 00:37:09.820
The breadth of topics is
a big surprise with this.

00:37:09.821 --> 00:37:12.865
Because there is no way for
us to actually prove that.

00:37:12.866 --> 00:37:16.550
There is no easy way for us to probe.

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:23.360
So I'll hand it over to
she'll tell you about.

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:27.805
&gt;&gt; Next, I will switch the gear
a little bit talking about

00:37:27.806 --> 00:37:31.525
some of the video
analysis work we have done.

00:37:31.526 --> 00:37:34.810
So this is also included
to the multimedia because

00:37:34.811 --> 00:37:39.100
video is one source
of the data format.

00:37:39.101 --> 00:37:44.200
So in here we just use action
segmentation as our task.

00:37:44.201 --> 00:37:46.720
This is a slide to show that

00:37:46.721 --> 00:37:49.330
action segmentation needs to be done

00:37:49.331 --> 00:37:52.510
at different granularity levels.

00:37:52.511 --> 00:37:55.495
To segment long videos,

00:37:55.496 --> 00:37:57.505
we need to do inference over

00:37:57.506 --> 00:38:02.965
long temporal span in order
to include the dependencies,

00:38:02.966 --> 00:38:07.315
causalities and also some temporal
ordering of the actions.

00:38:07.316 --> 00:38:08.710
At the same time,

00:38:08.711 --> 00:38:12.130
we need to provide
a sufficient of details to

00:38:12.131 --> 00:38:14.845
differentiate two similar actions

00:38:14.846 --> 00:38:17.860
even at very short temporal span.

00:38:17.861 --> 00:38:22.105
So to support these two requirements,

00:38:22.106 --> 00:38:26.590
actually we started to work on
this graph based representation.

00:38:26.591 --> 00:38:30.775
So we call it the Activity
Object Attribute graph.

00:38:30.776 --> 00:38:33.070
So basically it has two levels.

00:38:33.071 --> 00:38:34.585
On the top, we have the,

00:38:34.586 --> 00:38:36.385
we call it Activity graph.

00:38:36.386 --> 00:38:40.135
So it traces multiple
threats of activities.

00:38:40.136 --> 00:38:43.570
So this level we can
do inference over

00:38:43.571 --> 00:38:47.710
long temporal span to
include causality,

00:38:47.711 --> 00:38:51.055
dependencies and
ordering of the actions.

00:38:51.056 --> 00:38:52.480
On the bottom level,

00:38:52.481 --> 00:38:55.435
we have the object attribute graph.

00:38:55.436 --> 00:38:58.060
This where we have
detailed information about

00:38:58.061 --> 00:39:01.509
the objects associated
with the activity

00:39:01.510 --> 00:39:08.095
or action as well as their
attributes status or status changes.

00:39:08.096 --> 00:39:10.210
So with this level,

00:39:10.211 --> 00:39:13.150
with this amount of
detailed information,

00:39:13.151 --> 00:39:17.035
we can differentiate
two similar actions.

00:39:17.036 --> 00:39:21.250
Also, in our Object Attributes
graph as I mentioned,

00:39:21.251 --> 00:39:25.990
we also check the status change
of the objects or even the actor.

00:39:25.991 --> 00:39:28.840
In this way, we can not only infer

00:39:28.841 --> 00:39:33.880
the explicit observations but
also implicit consequences.

00:39:33.881 --> 00:39:35.965
So this is important for

00:39:35.966 --> 00:39:39.610
some applications where
the observations are noisy,

00:39:39.611 --> 00:39:42.970
we have missed detections or
even sometimes we don't have

00:39:42.971 --> 00:39:44.530
the observation at all because of

00:39:44.531 --> 00:39:48.670
the now overlapping
camera field of view.

00:39:48.671 --> 00:39:55.495
So this is a high level
illustration of our framework.

00:39:55.496 --> 00:39:59.020
On the bottom we have
the input image of frames.

00:39:59.021 --> 00:40:05.260
Then we extract all kinds of
descriptors including objects,

00:40:05.261 --> 00:40:08.425
attributes, actors, actions, motions

00:40:08.426 --> 00:40:12.355
and also sometimes we include
the seeing descriptors.

00:40:12.356 --> 00:40:16.180
Then based on this
spatial temporal graph,

00:40:16.181 --> 00:40:18.145
then we apply what we called

00:40:18.146 --> 00:40:21.910
the Stacked Spatial Temporal
Graph Collusional Network.

00:40:21.911 --> 00:40:24.895
I will give you more details later,

00:40:24.896 --> 00:40:31.840
but our network has
three major improvements.

00:40:31.841 --> 00:40:35.440
The first we allow arbitrary
temporal connections,

00:40:35.441 --> 00:40:38.410
so that we can account
for large amount

00:40:38.411 --> 00:40:43.015
of graph deformation and
also as I mentioned,

00:40:43.016 --> 00:40:46.555
we will use all kinds of
descriptors, objects,

00:40:46.556 --> 00:40:50.695
actions, things to deal
with complex activities.

00:40:50.696 --> 00:40:54.190
So we need a method to
support features or

00:40:54.191 --> 00:40:58.660
support nodes with
different feature length.

00:40:58.661 --> 00:41:02.095
&gt;&gt; Excuse me. What do
your nodes represent?

00:41:02.096 --> 00:41:06.820
&gt;&gt; So I think here we have
different kinds of nodes.

00:41:06.821 --> 00:41:12.160
You will see we have object nodes
here and also actor nodes.

00:41:12.161 --> 00:41:15.070
Actually, our implementation we also

00:41:15.071 --> 00:41:18.190
include the C-nodes and motion nodes.

00:41:18.191 --> 00:41:20.650
So these nodes it's not,

00:41:20.651 --> 00:41:24.835
it can't be like actors
specific though we will have

00:41:24.836 --> 00:41:28.780
all kinds of nodes do describe
different aspect of the actions.

00:41:28.781 --> 00:41:31.570
So it's not like we
are dividing the nodes

00:41:31.571 --> 00:41:34.945
per actor, but we will have-

00:41:34.946 --> 00:41:37.600
&gt;&gt; You know there's
no connection, right? It's not,

00:41:37.601 --> 00:41:39.475
table or chair.

00:41:39.476 --> 00:41:41.245
&gt;&gt; Yeah that's object nodes.

00:41:41.246 --> 00:41:44.230
So for actions you will
have object nodes.

00:41:44.231 --> 00:41:46.750
So if we go back here.

00:41:46.751 --> 00:41:53.110
On the top we have the this
is the activity nodes.

00:41:53.111 --> 00:41:54.760
So these are the actions.

00:41:54.761 --> 00:41:56.995
But under for each activities,

00:41:56.996 --> 00:42:00.470
we will have the actors
as well as the objects.

00:42:00.471 --> 00:42:04.575
So actually our implementation
we also add a scene.

00:42:04.576 --> 00:42:08.310
So all kinds of descriptors.

00:42:08.311 --> 00:42:11.815
So these nodes can be like a bunch of

00:42:11.816 --> 00:42:15.490
collection of the information
that is carried in the frame.

00:42:15.491 --> 00:42:17.280
&gt;&gt; And the edges?

00:42:17.281 --> 00:42:20.895
&gt;&gt; The edges, we have
two types of edges.

00:42:20.896 --> 00:42:23.070
So as you can see the solid edges

00:42:23.071 --> 00:42:25.230
here we call it the spatial edges.

00:42:25.231 --> 00:42:28.185
So this is just connect,

00:42:28.186 --> 00:42:33.355
some of the edges connect
the activities to the object.

00:42:33.356 --> 00:42:38.005
This will specify the role of
the object in the activities.

00:42:38.006 --> 00:42:42.445
So the dashed edges we call
it the temporal edges.

00:42:42.446 --> 00:42:45.475
So this is connected across frame.

00:42:45.476 --> 00:42:49.404
So we also, because in
the surveillance scenarios

00:42:49.405 --> 00:42:54.055
sometimes the cameras will not
have overlap to field views,

00:42:54.056 --> 00:42:57.790
so the red ones is still
the temporal connections,

00:42:57.791 --> 00:43:01.840
but that is sometimes we may
have missed detections or

00:43:01.841 --> 00:43:06.655
even don't have continuous observation
of the object or the actor.

00:43:06.656 --> 00:43:10.825
So these are the edges for the graph.

00:43:10.826 --> 00:43:12.325
&gt;&gt; You have a bunch of algorithm that

00:43:12.326 --> 00:43:14.710
extracts for you-all those, right?

00:43:14.711 --> 00:43:16.960
&gt;&gt; Yes. in the experiment,

00:43:16.961 --> 00:43:20.200
I will show you what algorithms

00:43:20.201 --> 00:43:24.200
we use to extract all
the descriptors of the nodes.

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:28.540
Then back to this,

00:43:28.541 --> 00:43:29.740
on top of that we use

00:43:29.741 --> 00:43:33.670
this hour glass structure to
improve the generalization,

00:43:33.671 --> 00:43:37.420
performance, localization
accuracy and

00:43:37.421 --> 00:43:43.300
also be able to handle the spatial
temporal at different scales.

00:43:43.301 --> 00:43:44.710
So I will have slides for

00:43:44.711 --> 00:43:47.890
these three improvements
in the following slides.

00:43:47.891 --> 00:43:52.480
&gt;&gt; Excuse me. What
does the arrow mean?

00:43:52.481 --> 00:43:54.325
So from the image,

00:43:54.326 --> 00:43:56.875
some features are
going to get computed-

00:43:56.876 --> 00:43:57.310
&gt;&gt; Yes

00:43:57.311 --> 00:44:01.510
&gt;&gt; -and something is going
to get updated at the nodes?

00:44:01.511 --> 00:44:02.410
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:44:02.411 --> 00:44:04.015
